      "proposed_proof": "**Given:** ZF and the Axiom of Choice; a compact oriented smooth sphere $M=S^3$ or $S^4$; $N\\ge2$; a smooth principal $SU(N)$-bundle $P$; a smooth connection $A$; and the finite-atlas $C^\\infty$ topology on $\\mathcal A_\\infty$.\n\n[A1] The Axiom of Choice means every family of nonempty sets has a choice function ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).\n\n[F1] The batch-56 configuration definition fixes $\\mathcal A_\\infty=A_0+\\Omega^1(M;\\operatorname{ad}P)$ with the finite-atlas $C^\\infty$ section topology and defines irreducibility by the based holonomy action on $\\mathbb C^N$. The complete definition was inspected; its Step 1 readiness record is missing, so its use here is conditional on that exact interface ([[def-qftx-smooth-sobolev-gauge-configuration]]).\n\n[F2] The batch-56 stabilizer lemma states that evaluation identifies the Sobolev stabilizer with the centralizer of $\\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A)$ and proves that an irreducible standard representation has centralizer $Z_N$. Its full statement and proof were inspected; its Step 1 readiness record is missing, so the stabilizer conclusion below remains conditional on that exact result ([[lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness]]).\n\n[F3] The union $\\mathscr G_N$ of the projection models for all proper nonzero complex subspaces of $\\mathbb C^N$ is compact ([[lem-qftx-complex-grassmannian-compactness]]).\n\n[F4] A continuous real-valued function on a nonempty compact space attains its minimum ([[thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps]], claim 2).\n\n[F5] A linear matrix ODE with continuous coefficients has a unique solution on a given compact interval ([[lem-linear-matrix-odes-have-unique-global-solutions-on-a-given-interval]]).\n\n\n\n**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 By [F1], $\\mathcal A_\\infty^*$ is exactly the set of connections whose holonomy action has no common proper nonzero invariant subspace. By [F2], for each such connection the stabilizer in the stated Sobolev gauge group is $Z_N$. The latter is the conditional stabilizer conclusion recorded separately from the holonomy criterion. [A1, given, F1, F2]\n\n1.2 For each $V\\in\\mathscr G_N$, irreducibility supplies a based piecewise-smooth loop $\\ell_V$ whose holonomy $g_V=\\operatorname{Hol}_A(\\ell_V)$ does not preserve $V$. If $P_V$ is the orthogonal projection onto $V$, then $(I-P_V)g_VP_V\\ne0$. For each fixed loop $\\ell$, the set of $V$ with $(I-P_V)\\operatorname{Hol}_A(\\ell)P_V\\ne0$ is open, since the projector topology and matrix multiplication are continuous. These open sets cover $\\mathscr G_N$. [F1, F3, given, construct]\n\n2.1 Compactness in [F3] gives a finite subcover, hence finitely many loops $\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_r$ for which no proper nonzero subspace is invariant under every $g_j=\\operatorname{Hol}_A(\\ell_j)$. The case $r=0$ cannot cover the nonempty space $\\mathscr G_N$, so $r\\ge1$. [F3, step 1.2, choose]\n\n3.1 Define the continuous function $$h_A(P)=\\sum_{j=1}^r\\|(I-P)g_jP\\|_F$$ on $\\mathscr G_N$, where $\\|\\cdot\\|_F$ is the Frobenius norm. It is positive at every $P$ by step 2.1. By [F4] it has a minimum $\\delta>0$, and $h_A(P)\\ge\\delta$ for every $P\\in\\mathscr G_N$. [F3, F4, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n3.2 On a smooth segment of any chosen loop, write parallel transport as $U'=-B(t)U$, where $B(t)\\in\\mathfrak{su}(N)$ is the connection form contracted with the path tangent; [F5] gives the unique solution on the whole segment. For a nearby connection with coefficient $B+b$, let $\\widetilde U$ be its solution and put $W=U^{-1}\\widetilde U$. Differentiation gives $W'=-U^{-1}bU\\,W$, $W(0)=I$, and skew-Hermiticity makes both $U$ and $W$ unitary. The integral equation therefore gives $\\|W(1)-I\\|_F\\le\\int\\|b(t)\\|_F\\,dt$. A $C^1$ perturbation of the connection makes each of the finitely many segment integrals arbitrarily small, so finite composition shows all $g_j$ vary continuously in the stated $C^1$ topology. Choose a neighborhood with $\\|g_j(A')-g_j(A)\\|_F<\\delta/(2r)$ for every $j$. [F5, step 2.1, algebra, choose]\n\n4.1 For any orthogonal projection $P$, both $\\|P\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}$ and $\\|I-P\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}$ are at most $1$. Thus $$ \\left|\\|(I-P)\\operatorname{Hol}_{A'}(\\ell_j)P\\|_F-\\|(I-P)g_jP\\|_F\\right| \\le\\|\\operatorname{Hol}_{A'}(\\ell_j)-g_j\\|_F. $$ Summing over $j$ and using step 3.2 gives $h_{A'}(P)>\\delta/2$ uniformly in $P$. Hence the same finite tuple has no common invariant proper nonzero subspace for every such $A'$. [step 3.1, step 3.2, algebra]\n\n5.1 A $C^1$ neighborhood on the compact base is specified by finitely many continuous section seminorms; each is continuous in the $C^\\infty$ section topology. Therefore the neighborhood in step 3.2 is also a $C^\\infty$ neighborhood, and $\\mathcal A_\\infty^*$ is open. Step 2.1 gives the finite holonomy witness at $A$, and step 1.1 gives the stated stabilizer. [F1, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.2, step 4.1] ∎"
